The Method Debugger
Method Debugger
Debug the source code which you wrote in the Method Editor in the Method Debugger while the Method is
being executed.
Remarks
To open the Method Debugger you can either:
• Press F11 in the Method Editor window.
• Select the command Debug Method on the Tools Ribbon Tab in the Method Editor window.
Note
If Plant Simulation is running in the foreground and is stuck in an endless loop, hold down Ctrl+Alt+left
Shift for several seconds to open the Debugger while a Method is being executed.
If Plant Simulation is not running in the foreground, hold down Ctrl+Alt+right Shift for several seconds
to open the Debugger while a Method is being executed.
Note
Plant Simulation opens the Debugger automatically when it encounters an error while executing the
source code or when it hits a breakpoint.
The Method Debugger
11-3670 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The Debugger window shows source code with the parameters and local variables of the Method. You can
set breakpoints at critical points and then check each step of the Method execution to see if the source
code does what you programmed it to do:
Remarks
Plant Simulation Help 11-3671
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Press F9 and select the context menu command or click the right mouse button while holding Shift
down to insert a Class Breakpoint, designated by a filled red circle 
  to the left of the line.
• Press Shift+F9 and select the context menu command or click the right mouse button while holding
Shift+Ctrl down to insert an Instance Breakpoint, designated by a red star 
  to the left of the line. It
only applies to this instance.
Once execution of the Methods reaches this Method, Plant Simulation opens the Method Debugger.
It shows the values of attributes and local variables as a Tooltip when you drag the mouse over the
instruction.
Double-click a word to select it. Triple-click in a line to select the entire line. Quadruple-click to select the
entire source code.
The Method highlights lines with faulty source code in red in the Method Debugger. It shows a message
telling you what’s wrong in the status bar of the Method Debugger window.
To restart executing the source code that follows after break point or an error, hold down Ctrl and
double-click that line.
If the Method Debugger was opened because of a runtime error and if you then close the Method
Debugger, Plant Simulation terminates all call chains without asking and stops the simulation.
If you only want to terminate the incorrect call chain and continue the simulation, press F5 instead of
closing the Method Debugger.
If you do not want to terminate the incorrect call chain, you can continue executing the incorrect Method
in a certain line of code by calling the context menu command Set Next Statement, or by placing the
input cursor into this line and by pressing Ctrl+F10, or by holding down Ctrl and double-clicking this line.
Then press F5 to continue executing the Method in this line.
The lower part of the Debugger window shows the Watch Window with the Tab Variables, the Tab
Anonymous Identifiers, the Tab Call Stack, the Tab Call Chains, the Tab Suspended, and the Tab
Expressions.
This is especially handy if you want to show the contents of two tabs of the Watch Window at the same
time. Then, you can click 
  and open a second watch window and click another tab there. You can
change its height by dragging the splitter.
Remarks
