Watch Window
Watch Window [described]
The Watch window shows information about the variables and anonymous identifiers used in your source
code.
Open the Watch Window
• Click 
  on the Toolbar of the Method Debugger
.
• Press the F12 key.
• Select the menu command Debug > Watch Window. By default, the Watch Window is part of the
Debugger window.
See also
@ [at sign, Method Debugger]
? (Question mark)
See also
Plant Simulation Help 11-3673
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Tab Variables [Watch window]
The tab Variables shows the names of all local variables and their values in their source code.
Remarks
If you double-click a variable, Plant Simulation opens the dialog Variable in which you can change the
value of the variable. Click OK to apply the new value and to close the dialog. Click Cancel to discard your
changes and to close the dialog.
The context menu shows the exact value with 17 floating point digits for local variables of data type
real, length, weight, speed, and acceleration. For values with a physical unit (length, weight, speed,
and acceleration) the value is shown in the respective SI unit, as Plant Simulation always keeps values
internally in SI units. If you selected another unit, Plant Simulation only converts the value for display
purposes when showing it. This has the advantage that Plant Simulation always computes with the same
numbers, independent of the selected unit, and that then the same rounding inaccuracies occur. Plant
Simulation thus always computes the same simulation results independent of the selected units.
See also
Local Variables
Tab Anonymous Indentifiers
Tab Anonymous Identifiers [Watch window]
The tab Anonymous Identifiers shows the anonymous identifiers @, ?, current, self, root, and
rootfolder.
Remarks
For user-defined attributes of data type Method Plant Simulation shows self.~ in addition.
For formulas and tables Plant Simulation shows xSelf and ySelf.
Tab Variables [Watch window]
11-3674 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The cells next to the anonymous identifiers @ and ? allow you to test the Method without running a
simulation. You only need to type something into these cells if you used @ and ? in your method.
If you type a path for @ or ? in the Method Debugger and type this anonymous identifier into a Method,
Plant Simulation will internally replace this anonymous identifier with the specified path.
The Watch Window shows changed values in red in the cells under Value.
See also
Anonymous Identifiers [described]
