Debug Menu
Debug Menu
The Debug menu provides the commands listed in the table of contents to the left.
You can select most of these settings for all Methods on the Debugger Ribbon Tab in the program
window.
Stop On
Opens a submenu on which you can select at which event the Method Debugger stops when executing a
Method.
Stop on Controls
The command Stop on Controls stops the simulation when the EventController encounters a Method—an
Entrance Control or Exit Control which you typed into an object—that is started from an object and
before executing the calling Method. Plant Simulation only executes calls by control methods during a
simulation run.
Instead, you can also click Stop on Controls 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the Method.
Stop on Formulas
The command Stop on Formulas stops the simulation and opens the Method Debugger whenever a
formula is evaluated.
Instead, you can also click Stop on Formulas 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the Method.
Debug Menu
11-3684 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Stop on Error Handler
The command Stop on Error Handler stops the simulation and opens the Method Debugger whenever an
error handling method is executed.
Instead, you can also click Stop on Error Handlers 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the Method.
Stop on Wakeup
The command Stop on Wakeup stops the simulation and opens the Method Debugger whenever the
execution of a suspended method is being continued.
Plant Simulation stops the simulation when waking up all suspended Methods that were suspended with
waituntil, stopuntil, wait, or sleep(true, false).
Instead, you can also click Stop on Wakeup 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the Method.
On Step Into [submenu]
Opens a submenu on which you select into which parts of the source code the Method Debugger steps
when debugging a Method.
Step into Controls
The command Step into Controls steps into other call chains to debug the executed Method. Such a
call chain can either be a control or a woken up suspended Method, the instruction @.move could, for
example, activate an Entrance Control.
Step into Formulas
The command Step into Formulas steps into other formulas to debug the executed Method. The
instruction print table[1,1] could, for example, compute a formula which is located in this cell.
Stop on Error Handler
Plant Simulation Help 11-3685
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Step into Encrypted Methods
The command Step into Encrypted Methods sets if the command Step Into (F11) stops in encrypted
Methods or not.
• By default Step into Encrypted Methods is deactivated, meaning that the command Step Into does not
stop in encrypted Methods.
• If you activate Step into Encrypted Methods, Step Into also stops in encrypted Methods.
The Method-Debugger then shows the message The source code is encrypted instead of the source
code as such.
Step into Encrypted Methods
11-3686 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Ignore
Opens a submenu on which you can select which types of events the Method Debugger ignores when
executing a Method.
Ignore Breakpoints
The command Ignore Breakpoints ignores the user-defined breakpoints you set with the commands
Toggle Class Breakpoint and Toggle Instance Breakpoint.
Instead, you can also click Ignore Breakpoints 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the Method.
SimTalk
ignoreBreakpoints
Ignore Error Handlers
The command Ignore Error Handlers does not execute error handling methods if a runtime error in a
Method occurs while it is being executed.
Instead, Plant Simulation opens the Method Debugger at the position in the source code at which the
runtime error occurred.
Instead, you can also click Ignore Error Handlers 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the
Method.
SimTalk
ignoreBreakpoints [SimTalk]
Ignore
Plant Simulation Help 11-3687
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Ignore Errors
The command Ignore Errors stops the simulation after an error occurred in the current Method. Plant
Simulation opens the Method Debugger with the Method containing the error and highlights the line
containing the error in red.
Note
The simulation might continue if additional events are contained in the Event List in the
EventController. This way you can continue working, although your model contains methods with faulty
source code.
If an error occurs, Plant Simulation shows an error message in the status bar. It contains a description of
the error, the Method, the line number of the error, and the active call chain
Instead, you can also click Ignore Errors 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the Method.
SimTalk
setErrorStop [SimTalk]
Ignore Errors in Formulas
The command Ignore Errors in Formulas prevents opening the Method Debugger when a runtime error
occurs in a formula.
Instead, you can also click Ignore Errors in Formulas 
  on the Debugger Ribbon Tab of the
Method.
Step Over
Executes the source code of the Method one line at a time. Method execution stops before the next line of
source code.
Remarks
Other Methods that are being called in that line of the source code are not loaded into the Method
Debugger.
Step Over
11-3688 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Or click 
  on the Toolbar of the Method Debugger
.
The Method Debugger shows the elapsed time when you execute one or several instructions in a Method
with the commands Step Over, Step Into, Step Out, or Run to Cursor and if executing the instructions
takes 10 milliseconds or longer.
Video on YouTube
https://youtu.be/pbOQD0p4hO4?si=P0Frai1pltBZLxAD&t=614
Step Into
Executes one line of the source code of the debugged Method at a time.
Remarks
If the line happens to call other methods, Plant Simulation loads this Method into the Method Debugger as
well. Method execution stops before the next line in the called Method.
The command Step Into does not jump into encrypted Methods. If the next instruction is the call of
an encrypted Method, the command Step Into behaves like the command Step Over, meaning that it
executes the source code of the Method line by line.
Video on YouTube
Plant Simulation Help 11-3689
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Or you can click 
  on the Toolbar of the Method Debugger
.
Note
Step Into and Step Over do not differ if the next line of the source code to be executed does not contain
a method call.
The Method Debugger shows the elapsed time when you execute one or several instructions in a Method
with the commands Step Over, Step Into, Step Out, or Run to Cursor and if executing the instructions
takes 10 milliseconds or longer.
Video on YouTube
https://youtu.be/5t-wLNmKpbU?si=T8hXaB_8xak8P6Rv&t=453
Step Out
Finishes executing the current Method and steps out of it. It then stops at the next statement of the calling
Method.
Remarks
Or click 
  on the Toolbar of the Method Debugger
.
Video on YouTube
